The runbook lists 71 known duplicates for D9. The report shows only 23 removed, so 48 duplicates are probably still in the data. Removing 71 would leave 8,032 rows, not 8,080.

The other checks match the runbook. There are 46 columns, and the weights sum to the 5,330,850 target. The female share also matches census. None of those checks would catch leftover duplicates. The row-count check passes only because it compares against the raw file, not against the expected duplicate count.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "Only 23 duplicates were removed, but the runbook says D9 has 71 known duplicates, so about 48 remain; the other checks can't detect this."}

The work item failed. The inner merge with respondents_meta.csv cut the data from 36,528 rows to 5,338. The item said nothing else may be dropped, and that merge dropped 31,190 rows.

The expected row count after deduplication is 37,897 − 1,369 = 36,528. The output has only 5,338 rows. The validation check passed the row count only because it compared against the raw count minus duplicates, which is a flawed check. The weights were then rescaled to 4,076,872 over the surviving rows, so the weight total looks correct even though most respondents are gone.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.97, "reason": "An inner merge with respondents_meta dropped 31,190 rows (36,528 -> 5,338), violating 'nothing else may be dropped'; the weight total and other checks hide this."}

The invoice went to the wrong recipient. The runbook says invoices go to the billing contact, and for K-29 that is j.gruber@berger-moebel.at. The mail was sent to s.kraus@berger-moebel.at, who is the sales contact.

The due date is correct. 2026-10-23 plus 60 days is 2026-12-22, which matches the K-29 terms. The PDF and logo were attached, and the message was delivered to mx1.berger-moebel.at after an initial greylist deferral. None of that fixes the recipient.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.85, "reason": "Invoice was delivered to K-29's sales contact (s.kraus@) instead of the billing contact j.gruber@berger-moebel.at required by the runbook."}

Weights sum to 6,988,427, but the target population is 8,831,656. The recalibration used the wrong total.

Everything else matches: 15 duplicates removed, 16,997 rows, 64 columns. The weighting is the failure.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.96, "reason": "Weights were rescaled to 6,988,427 instead of the target population 8,831,656, so recalibration is wrong despite correct dedupe and schema."}

Expected rows after deduplication: 8,200 − 1,191 = 7,009. The output has only 938 rows, so about 6,071 more rows were dropped than the duplicate removal justifies. The work item said nothing else may be dropped.

The other checks pass. The weight sum of 8,906,103 matches the target population, and there are 30 columns, as expected. Respondent IDs are unique. The weights still look fine because they were recalibrated on the surviving rows, so the weight sum can't reveal the missing rows.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.96, "reason": "Output has 938 rows, but removing 1,191 duplicates from 8,200 should leave 7,009; many non-duplicate rows were dropped even though the weights sum correctly."}

