The runbook has a target population for D4 of 7,271,124. The report shows the sum of weights as 9,014,753, so the weights were not recalibrated to the target.

The other checks pass. The row count is right: 22,362 − 34 = 22,328, which matches the 34 known duplicates. The schema has 59 columns, as expected. The weighted female share matches the census. A weight total that is wrong can still give the right proportions, so the female share doesn't show that the recalibration worked.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Sum of weights is 9,014,753 but the D4 target population is 7,271,124, so the weights were not recalibrated to the target."}

Weights sum to 9,014,753, but the D4 target population is 7,271,124. The weights were not recalibrated to the target.

Everything else checks out. The 34 duplicates match the runbook. The row count is correct at 22,362 − 34 = 22,328. The schema has 59 columns, as expected. The gender share is fine. The weight step is the only failure.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Weights sum to 9,014,753 instead of the D4 target population of 7,271,124, so recalibration is wrong, though dedup and schema are correct."}

The amount is wrong. The work item says €1,632.88, but the email, the payment link and the ledger all show €163.29. The sums differ by roughly a factor of 10.

The other details match. The recipient is correct, the PDF and logo are attached, and the due date of 2026-11-22 is 30 days after 2026-10-23. The dunning reminder on 2026-11-29 is 7 days after the due date, as the runbook says. The invoice was delivered, but with the wrong amount, and the ledger holds the same wrong amount.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.93, "reason": "Invoice was delivered, but the email, payment link and ledger show €163.29 instead of €1,632.88."}

The job failed its main requirement. Only duplicates were supposed to be dropped, but the inner merge with respondents_meta.csv cut the data from 28,643 rows to 4,639. That removed 24,004 respondents who weren't duplicates.

The other checks pass. The weights sum to 1,665,361, the file has 59 columns, and the 64 duplicates were removed. The expected output was 28,643 rows. The weights look fine only because they were rescaled over the surviving rows, so the check on their total doesn't show the loss.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.97, "reason": "Inner merge with respondents_meta dropped 28,643 rows to 4,639, so non-duplicate respondents were lost, violating 'nothing else may be dropped'."}

The work item failed. The inner merge with respondents_meta.csv cut the data from 28,643 rows to 4,639. That drops 24,004 rows that were not duplicates, and the task said nothing else may be dropped.

The other checks pass, but they don't catch this. The weights sum to 1,665,361, the schema has 59 columns, and the output has 4,639 unique respondent_ids. The validation report only restates the row count of 4,639 and doesn't compare it with the expected 28,643. Rescaling the weights to the target on the shrunken set hides the loss.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.96, "reason": "An inner merge with respondents_meta dropped 28,643 → 4,639 rows (about 24,000 non-duplicate respondents lost), violating 'nothing else may be dropped'."}

The output has 64 columns, but the raw file had 68 and the task said nothing else may be dropped. The runbook also lists 68 columns for D8. So four columns were lost.

Everything else checks out. The 106 duplicates were removed, leaving 20,094 rows. The weights sum to 6,679,126. The weighted female share matches the census figure. The validation report only checks that "64 columns present", so it did not catch the loss.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Output has 64 columns versus 68 in the raw file and runbook, so four columns were dropped despite the 'nothing else may be dropped' requirement."}

Rows: 20,200 − 106 = 20,094 ✓. The weight sum is 6,679,126, which matches the target ✓. Respondent IDs are unique ✓. But the output has 64 columns, and the runbook expects 68 for D8. Four columns are missing, so something besides the duplicates was dropped. The task said nothing else may be dropped.

The weights and rows are right, but the column loss violates the "nothing else dropped" requirement.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.8, "reason": "Rows and weight total are correct, but the output has 64 columns where the runbook expects 68, so four columns were dropped."}

Weights sum to 6,109,908, but the runbook gives a D3 target population of 7,683,054. The weights were not recalibrated to the target.

Other checks match the runbook. 366 duplicates were removed, giving 32,478 − 366 = 32,112 rows. The file has 72 columns and was written to the right path. The validation report only compares the weight sum with the job's own number (6,109,908), not with the target, so its green check doesn't catch the problem.

{"works": false, "confidence": 0.9, "reason": "Weights sum to 6,109,908 rather than the D3 target population of 7,683,054, so recalibration was done to the wrong total even though dedupe and schema are correct."}

